{"hash":"0acc31043d0ad68e","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":68.662,"said":"Turn round here, before the Mamleshwar end of the bridge, and look back at the island. Mandhata is a hill standing in the middle of the river. The Narmada runs along this side of it. On the far side her smaller arm, the Kaveri, flows round it, and beyond the island’s western tip the two meet again. People say that the island’s shape, seen from above, is like the sacred syllable written in Devanagari: ॐ, Om. You cannot see it from here, and even from the air it is a likeness the eye has to look for, in the curve of the river and the folds of the hill, not a drawing. But that is what the pilgrims hold dear: the Lord of Om dwells on Om itself, and the river traces it around him day and night. The Mandukya Upanishad begins: Om, this syllable, is all this world. And so, the pilgrims say, when you walk the parikrama path round Mandhata, you are walking round Om.\n\nDid you know? The island is about four kilometres long and some 2.6 square kilometres in area, and is reached by boat or by bridge.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":868},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":884,"end":1001}],"words":[[0.098,0.276,0,4],[0.387,0.312,5,10],[0.711,0.325,11,15],[1.3,0.361,17,23],[1.673,0.072,24,27],[1.757,0.673,28,38],[2.442,0.204,39,42],[2.658,0.096,43,45],[2.766,0.096,46,49],[2.874,0.397,50,56],[3.524,0.18,58,61],[3.716,0.18,62,66],[3.908,0.312,67,71],[4.233,0.132,72,74],[4.377,0.132,75,78],[4.521,0.493,79,85],[5.926,0.625,87,95],[6.562,0.144,96,98],[6.719,0.084,99,100],[6.815,0.18,101,105],[7.007,0.553,106,114],[7.572,0.108,115,117],[7.692,0.084,118,121],[7.788,0.385,122,128],[8.185,0.132,129,131],[8.329,0.084,132,135],[8.425,0.433,136,141],[9.736,0.12,143,146],[9.868,0.493,147,154],[10.373,0.349,155,159],[10.733,0.312,160,165],[11.058,0.18,166,170],[11.25,0.252,171,175],[11.514,0.156,176,178],[11.683,0.276,179,181],[12.873,0.156,183,185],[13.041,0.084,186,189],[13.137,0.325,190,19